>
  <p>
    Someone whose contact lenses ran out in Prosper, or a parent in Frisco whose child is squinting at the board,
    searches “eye exam near me” and looks at the map. She checks who takes her vision plan, reads recent reviews and
    books the office that can see her soon. More of them now ask ChatGPT or read Google’s AI answer first.
  </p>
  <p>
    Independent optometrists compete with retail chains that advertise heavily. The independent practice usually offers
    more: medical eye care, children’s exams, specialty lenses. The search results rarely show it. Here is where most
    practices lose patients, and what fixes it.
  </p>

  <h2>Where eye care practices lose patients</h2>
  <h3>Categories that hide the medical work</h3>
  <p>
    “Optometrist” as the primary category, then the secondary categories that fit: eye care center, optician,
    contact lenses supplier. List each service on the profile too. A practice that looks like an eyewear shop is not
    found by the patient with dry eye or a red, painful eye.
  </p>
  <h3>One page for every service</h3>
  <p>
    Patients search “pediatric eye exam Plano”, “dry eye treatment McKinney”, “contact lens exam Allen”. A single
    services page ranks for none of those. Each core service needs its own page: what it is, who it suits, what the
    visit involves, and how vision plans and medical insurance apply.
  </p>
  <h3>Vision plan and medical insurance left unclear</h3>
  <p>
    Patients are often unsure whether a visit falls under a vision plan or medical insurance. Say on the site which
    plans you accept and explain the difference in plain words. It saves your front desk calls and keeps patients from
    choosing someone who answered first.
  </p>
  <h3>Reviews that stopped</h3>
  <p>
    Patients read the newest reviews first. A practice with a long history and nothing recent loses to one with a
    steady flow from this season. Ask at checkout or when glasses are picked up, with a direct link, and reply to every
    review.
  </p>
  <h3>No way to book after hours</h3>
  <p>
    Many patients search in the evening. An online booking or appointment-request link on the profile and on every
    service page catches them before they book with the chain that has one.
  </p>
  <h3>Nothing for AI to quote</h3>
  <p>
    AI assistants recommend practices they can confirm across sources. Clear service pages, consistent listings and
    recent, specific reviews are what they read. Our guide to <a href="/visibility/ai-search/">AI search</a> covers it
    in full.
  </p>

  <h2>Eye care rules to respect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Keep patient details out of review replies. Thank them; do not confirm they are a patient.</li>
    <li>Do not promise outcomes for any condition. Describe the exam and the care you offer.</li>
    <li>Keep advertised prices and offers accurate, with any conditions stated plainly.</li>
    <li>Never offer discounts or gifts in exchange for reviews.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What the Visibility Audit covers for an eye care practice</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Your map position for your top services, across the towns you draw from</li>
    <li>Your Google profile against the three practices that show up instead of you</li>
